      "input": "nums = [0]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [0, 1, 0, 1]",
      "output": "4",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Turn 'the same number of 0s and 1s' into a running total that must be equal at two different positions.",
    "Map 1 to +1 and 0 to -1: a slice is balanced exactly when the running total just before the slice equals the running total just after it.",
    "Store the first index at which each running total appeared, including a sentinel for the total 0 before the array starts, and take the largest index gap for any repeated total."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes a left endpoint `i`, sweeps the right endpoint `j` to the right while counting how many ones the slice contains, and accepts the slice when its length is even and the ones are exactly half that length, keeping the largest accepted length. That is O(n^2) time with O(1) extra memory. The optimal version rewrites 'equal numbers of 0s and 1s' as an arithmetic statement about a running total: score a 1 as +1 and a 0 as -1 and take prefix sums. A slice is balanced exactly when the prefix total immediately before it equals the prefix total immediately after it, so the answer is the largest gap between two positions carrying the same running total. A hash map from total to the earliest index where that total occurred gives, for each step, either a previously stored start or a new record to store, in one pass.",
    "why_optimal": "Each element must be read at least once to be counted, so O(n) time is a lower bound, and the map attains it: one lookup and at most one insertion per index. The extra O(n) memory is the price of the one-pass view; note that the constraint allows at most 100 entries, so the brute force is also acceptable here, while the map version is the one that survives an array of millions of entries or a wider alphabet of symbols that must balance.",
    "pitfalls": "Seeding the map empty, or storing index 0 for total 0, instead of mapping total 0 to the sentinel index -1: every balanced slice that begins at index 0 is then lost. Overwriting a stored index with a later occurrence, which shrinks every future span, because only the earliest occurrence of a total can produce the longest slice. Flipping the sign convention partway through, so the running total stops repeating and the map never returns a hit. Initialising the answer to -1 or 1, which leaks an impossible length for inputs such as `[1]`; the answer really can be 0 and must be initialised as such. Recording the total for a repeated value twice, which is harmless but hides the earliest-index rule that the algorithm depends on."
  },
